From 2ecefa41a88dc4092e3626d0333c32f9a04be467 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:14:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: keep libplacebo's GPU programs from one session to
 the next

No pl_cache was given to libplacebo: every program of the Dolby Vision
bridge (one per shader variant: enhancement layer, bars written first,
composed or direct, HDR10) was compiled and linked from its source when it
was first drawn, at the start of every Kodi session and in the middle of
videos when a new variant came.

dvbridge_cache.c keeps a pl_cache in a file, which pl_gpu_set_cache()
gives to every pl_gpu of the bridge (native and HDR10 output share it).
With it libplacebo stores the binary of each program it links (keyed by
the GL vendor, renderer and version and the program's source) and loads it
with glProgramBinary() the next time; a binary that the driver does not
take is compiled from its source as before. The file is named by a hash
of the GL strings, the system's name and version and libplacebo's
version, under special://temp/dvbridge/; the four most recently written
files are kept. It is read when a bridge is set up and written when native
output ends and when a bridge goes, only when something was added: into a
file of its own, renamed over the old one. Before libplacebo reads a file,
its header and the size of every entry are checked against the file
(libplacebo allocates the size an entry declares, and aborts when it
cannot); a file that does not hold is deleted, and an entry whose
checksum fails is left out with the ones after it.

Tested on the host: a cache test with files cut, padded, with another magic
or version, a 1 TiB entry, an entry size that wraps, a key 0, changed
bytes, keys that xor to 0, a bound smaller than an entry, an unwritable
folder (31 checks); and the render test of the FEL test stream through
zink on lavapipe (which gives program binaries only with Mesa's disk cache
on): cold, 16 programs written; from the file, all 21 programs of the run
taken from it and none compiled, and the hashes of all 36 drawings the
same as without any cache. Measured there: 21 programs took 89 ms to
compile with nothing cached and 7.6 ms with only Mesa's own disk cache
(which LibreELEC builds and keeps in /storage/.cache); what the binaries
save on a real driver is not measured.
